  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1053 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nMOSTLY SUNNY AND DRY. ISOLATED RAIN OVER THE SAN JUANS:\n\nModels continue to highlight some very isolated convection each\nafternoon, mainly over the San Juans and central mountains,\nthrough the end of the week. Enough moisture will remain under \nthe high pressure to allow daytime heating to kick off this\nconvection. Tuesday and Wednesday, weak disturbances will move\nthrough causing an increase in convective coverage. Some of the\nthe showers and storms may be a little stronger but dynamics and\nlift remain fairly weak so not too concerned. Look for some \ngusty winds and lightning, typical in this type of environment.\nAs we remain under high pressure, daytime highs will reach \nanywhere from 5 to near 15 degrees above more seasonal values. \n\nPRECIP CHANCES INCREASE LATE WEEKEND:\n\nConfidence continues to increase for a pattern change and a\nreturn to more widespread precipitation late this weekend. A\nclosed low is still progged to drop down from the PacNW and as \nthis occurs, southwesterly flow will allow moisture to increase \nacross the desert southwest. The end result will be isolated to \nscattered showers/storms late Sunday night, increasing in \ncoverage as the low reaches the NV border and starts tracking \neastward. As of now, the heaviest precip looks to occur Monday \nevening and overnight. Having said that, any changes to the low \ntrack, available moisture, and upper level support could change \nthe going forecast. Suffice to say, clouds and rain will be \nmaking a return with the finer details still a bit hazy. Keep an\neye on the latest forecast for all the updates.",
